{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015nkZvYhcMA7DEGNEuChaRP","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (79KB). Full output saved to: /home/testpwn/.claude/projects/-home-testpwn-huntress-2026/238238c1-2779-4c5f-8776-376f2b230b3f/tool-results/b1ykc426v.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n# Warmups coverage atlas\n\nLane: one `message` question over `blob.txt`. Plaintext is ALWAYS the fixed sentence\n`Nice work decoding this warmup! To solve, submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <adj>-<noun>-<noun>-NN`\n(87 chars + slug, ~105-115 bytes). One new keyless codec per day, named by a pun in the title; Mastery line k = day k's\ncodec over a mid-word fragment (`NN<TAB>fragment`). D16-D17 were STACKED: a symbol carrier over HEX (D16 Morse, D17\nBaconian a/b 5-groups whose values 0-15 are the nibbles), both cued by \"Sixteen\". D18 went back to ONE layer and opened\na new family, TRANSPOSITION: rail fence, 3 rails, offset 0 (\"Three rails. One very zigzag commute.\" / \"Off the Rails\");\nthe prompt's number was the key. D19 (\"Two bytes walk into a bar. The little one goes first.\" / \"Little Things\") was\nHEX of the UTF-16LE bytes with a `fffe` BOM: two layers again, the inner one a TEXT ENCODING (byte representation), the\npun naming code-unit width (\"two bytes\") and byte order (\"little ... first\"). D20 (\"Same bar. This time the big one goes\nfirst.\" / \"Big Entrance\") was the SAME codec with ONE knob turned: hex of UTF-16BE with a `feff` BOM -- a callback pun\n(\"Same bar\") plus the changed parameter (\"big\"); the BOM-aware D19 solver passed it unchanged. Expect more \"same X,\none knob\" days: the third byte order (middle/PDP-endian), four bytes (UTF-32, prebuilt), no BOM (prebuilt), the whole\ntext as one BIG integer, or a knob on an older day's codec (code-unit escapes byte-swapped, bit order, alphabet).\nD21 (\"A familiar wrapper. Something compressed inside.\" / \"Under Pressure\") opened a new family, COMPRESSION: GZIP\ninside standard padded BASE64 (`H4sIAAAAAAACA...`, header `1f8b0800 00000000 0203`); Mastery line 21 = each 36-char\nfragment its own gzip member. \"Familiar wrapper\" = an already-seen codec as the OUTER layer. Expect the D19->D20\npattern again (\"same wrapper, other squeeze\") -- and D22 delivered it: \"Another familiar wrapper. This one needs a little\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"238238c1-2779-4c5f-8776-376f2b230b3f","uuid":"61e5f585-b278-47e4-bdfa-f5b13e68d55f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:05:12.240Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Warmups coverage atlas\n\nLane: one `message` question over `blob.txt`. Plaintext is ALWAYS the fixed sentence\n`Nice work decoding this warmup! To solve, submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <adj>-<noun>-<noun>-NN`\n(87 chars + slug, ~105-115 bytes). One new keyless codec per day, named by a pun in the title; Mastery line k = day k's\ncodec over a mid-word fragment (`NN<TAB>fragment`). D16-D17 were STACKED: a symbol carrier over HEX (D16 Morse, D17\nBaconian a/b 5-groups whose values 0-15 are the nibbles), both cued by \"Sixteen\". D18 went back to ONE layer and opened\na new family, TRANSPOSITION: rail fence, 3 rails, offset 0 (\"Three rails. One very zigzag commute.\" / \"Off the Rails\");\nthe prompt's number was the key. D19 (\"Two bytes walk into a bar. The little one goes first.\" / \"Little Things\") was\nHEX of the UTF-16LE bytes with a `fffe` BOM: two layers again, the inner one a TEXT ENCODING (byte representation), the\npun naming code-unit width (\"two bytes\") and byte order (\"little ... first\"). D20 (\"Same bar. This time the big one goes\nfirst.\" / \"Big Entrance\") was the SAME codec with ONE knob turned: hex of UTF-16BE with a `feff` BOM -- a callback pun\n(\"Same bar\") plus the changed parameter (\"big\"); the BOM-aware D19 solver passed it unchanged. Expect more \"same X,\none knob\" days: the third byte order (middle/PDP-endian), four bytes (UTF-32, prebuilt), no BOM (prebuilt), the whole\ntext as one BIG integer, or a knob on an older day's codec (code-unit escapes byte-swapped, bit order, alphabet).\nD21 (\"A familiar wrapper. Something compressed inside.\" / \"Under Pressure\") opened a new family, COMPRESSION: GZIP\ninside standard padded BASE64 (`H4sIAAAAAAACA...`, header `1f8b0800 00000000 0203`); Mastery line 21 = each 36-char\nfragment its own gzip member. \"Familiar wrapper\" = an already-seen codec as the OUTER layer. Expect the D19->D20\npattern again (\"same wrapper, other squeeze\") -- and D22 delivered it: \"Another familiar wrapper. This one needs a little\ninflation.\" / \"Deflated Ego\" = ZLIB (`78da`, level 9) inside std padded BASE64 (`eNo..`/`eNp..`/`eNr..`); Mastery line\n22 = each 20-char fragment its own zlib stream. The D21 solver's `_b64z` (magic sniff incl. `\\x78`) passed it unchanged.\nRHYTHM since D19: day N opens a family, day N+1 turns ONE knob (D19 LE -> D20 BE, D21 gzip -> D22 zlib). D23-D25 each\nopened a NEW family instead: D23 \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe might recognize the neighbors.\" = ASCII85 `<~...~>`\n(Mastery line 23 own delimiters); D24 \"Count carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\" / \"String Theory\" = DJB\nNETSTRINGS `1:N,1:i,...` (one char per frame, payload `,`/`:` framed as `1:,,`/`1::,`; line 24 `1:o,1:n,...`); D25\n\"Every character has a point. A code point, anyway.\" / \"You Have a Point\" = Unicode CODE POINTS `U+004E U+0069 ...`\n(UPPERCASE, 4 hex digits, single spaces, 762 B = 7 B per char; Mastery line 25 = 5 tokens per fragment), one layer,\nplain text inside -- a return to the D7-D9 CHARACTER-NOTATION family (D9 was `\"\\u00XX\"`), predicted here as row 22\n(prebuilt `uplus_dec`, single CORRECT ~27 s). Only a \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" prompt has signalled a knob day.\nD26+ forecast, in order: (a) the code-point KNOB or a sibling notation -- Unicode character NAMES (\"every character has\na name\": `LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N`, `\\N{...}`), brace/decimal/mixed escapes, code points SHIFTED into another block\n(Tags-block ASCII smuggling U+E00xx, fullwidth, math), code-point arithmetic (constant offset, deltas, XOR), variation-\nselector byte smuggling; (b) a \"point\" re-read: floating/fixed point numbers carrying char codes, dot-matrix /\nASCII-art letters; (c) another new family entirely (script letter maps: runes/Greek/Cyrillic; mojibake/double\nencoding). All keyless; charset fingerprint + crib slug gate is the job. Snapshot 2026-10-25 (after D25; EVERY earlier\nprebuild gap is built, incl. the whole D24 framing queue; new Unicode/notation queue below, rows 173-182).\n\nStatus legend: `seen Dn` = GM-confirmed day codec (`lib.warmups_codecs.DAYS`, `lib.decode.decode_chunk`/`FREE_CODECS`).\n`prebuilt tgt:<id>` = speculative race module + fixtures from `tests/data/warmups_fixtures/TARGETS.json` (KEPT in\n`.run/prebuild-state.json`). `prebuilt wc:<name>` = encoder/decoder pair in `lib.warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES` (used by\n`infer`/`plaintext`/today-probe; free-text ones also `SPECULATIVE_FREE`). `prebuilt dec:<fn>` = `lib.decode` branch.\n`gap` = nothing in lib (a standard tool may still decode it: chepy 7.x and CyberChef at `tools/npm-cyberchef` are\ninstalled; `.venv` has `base58`, `pycipher`, `Crypto`; NOT installed: base45/base91/pybase62/pybase100/z85/unidecode).\n\n**Crib fact (all keyed rows):** the first 87 plaintext chars are known, so ANY fixed-key letter cipher leaks its key\nfrom the blob alone: Vigenere/Beaufort/Gronsfeld/autokey key = (c - p) / (p - c) / (k - c) over the crib, period =\nsmallest repeat; keyword substitution = the crib map (21 of 26 letters; only f j q y z unseen, slug-only).\n\"Keyless?\" below therefore means \"decodable without outside info\": title pun OR crib.\n\n## Coverage table\n\n| # | concept | status | keyless? | recognition cues | standard decode | D19+ likelihood |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | base64 (std, padded) | seen D1 