{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgQuT9j5K4umSTafKvAWz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I updated both files: `reference/techniques/warmups.md` and `reference/coverage/warmups.md`. Day 26 was a Brainfuck program, and the solver in `solver/` still times out on Mastery.\n\n**What day 26 was.** \"Food for Thought\" (\"A very small language has quite a lot to say\") was a Brainfuck program using only `+ - .`. It uses one cell. Each character is written as a run of `+` or `-` equal to the change from the previous character, then a `.`. The single solve was CORRECT in about 27 s and the Time Trial in 3.9 s.\n\n**What went wrong in Mastery.** The solver timed out at 64.9 s with no answers, and a live worker decoded the 128 sets by hand to reach CORRECT. The generalize workers were stopped before they found the cause. My diagnosis comes from reading `lib/decode.py`, not from a profile. The solver registered a day-26 encoder that writes each character from zero rather than as a change from the previous one, as the challenge's own text does. The routing check re-encodes line 26 and compares it to the original. That check failed on every set, so each set fell into the slow fallback search. Tomorrow's copied solver will hit the same timeout unless that registration is replaced; the fix is the top priority-1 note in the atlas (`mastery-bf-line-26`).\n\n**Changes:**\n1. **Technique log:**\n   - Added a day-26 entry and folded day 25 into the days 14–25 summary.\n   - Added a variant axis for the Brainfuck format, with values seen and a parse rule.\n   - Added a recognition row with a one-liner that I checked against the real artifact.\n   - Added pitfalls: a registered encoder must reproduce the real Mastery bytes, and programs only ever run in a step-limited Python interpreter.\n2. **Coverage atlas:**\n   - Row 83 (Brainfuck/Ook!) is now `seen D26`. It had been rated low as \"program, not encoding\".\n   - Added rows 183–191 for other tiny languages and rewrote the forecast: Ook!-style Brainfuck dialects are most likely next, then other small languages.\n   - Ten Unicode rows that are already built are now marked prebuilt.\n   - The cues section is exactly at the 60-line limit.\n3. **Readiness gaps:** I removed the old Brainfuck/esoteric gap. Every earlier prebuild gap had already been built, so I added 9 new unbuilt ones, priority 1 first: Brainfuck dialects, full Brainfuck programs, Deadfish, Whitespace. The rest cover Befunge, JSFuck, Chef, toy instruction listings and Malbolge. The JSON block is valid and keeps the same keys.\n\nThe technique log is 266 lines, longer than the ~200 target."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1161,"cache_read_input_tokens":141645,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1161},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"238238c1-2779-4c5f-8776-376f2b230b3f","uuid":"b3456122-f955-47aa-a009-bcf308dfca6f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:11:35.561Z","request_id":"req_011CgQuT9MkVAzE1mAVzcJ3x"}
